 refusal and noncontact nonresponse in household surveys: evidence from six UK government surveys. Southampton, UK: University of Southampton, 2007 (available from <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://eprints.soton.ac.uk/46013/">http://eprints.soton.ac.uk/46013/</ext-link>).</citation></ref><ref id="ref21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal">McGovern PG, Lurie N, Margolis KL, Slater JS. Accuracy of self-report of mammography and Pap smear in a low-income urban population. <source>Am J Prev Med</source><year>1998</year>;<volume>14</volume>:<fpage>201</fpage>-8.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9569221</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal">Bowman JA, Sanson-Fisher R, Redman S. The accuracy of self-reported Pap smear utilisation. <source>Soc Sci Med</source><year>1997</year>;<volume>44</volume>:<fpage>969</fpage>-76.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9089918</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref23"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal">Canfell K, Beral V, Green J, Cameron R, Baker K, Brown A. The agreement between self-reported cervical smear abnormalities and screening programme records. <source>J Med Screen</source><year>2006</year>;<volume>13</volume>:<fpage>72</fpage>-5.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16792828</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><notes notes-type="citation-text"><p>Cite this as: <italic>BMJ</italic> 2009;338:b2025</p></notes></back></article>